# Git Rebase Workflow

## 1. Switch to Your Working Branch

```bash
git checkout branch_name
```

---

## 2. Get the Latest Changes from GitHub

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
```

This updates your local `main` branch.

---

## 3. Switch Back to Your Branch

```bash
git checkout branch_name
```

---

## 4. Rebase Your Branch onto Main

```bash
git rebase main
```

This moves your branch's commits on top of the latest `main` branch.

---

## 5. If There Are Merge Conflicts

Check the conflicted files:

```bash
git status
```

Fix the conflicts in the files, then run:

```bash
git add .
git rebase --continue
```

If more conflicts appear, repeat the same steps.

---

## 6. Cancel the Rebase (Optional)

If you want to stop the rebase and return to the previous state:

```bash
git rebase --abort
```

---

## 7. Push the Rebased Branch

Since the commit history has changed, use:

```bash
git push --force-with-lease origin branch_name
```

---

# Complete Rebase Workflow

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main

git checkout branch_name
git rebase main

# If conflicts occur
git add .
git rebase --continue

# Push after rebase
git push --force-with-lease origin branch_name
```

---

# Simple Flow

**Update main → Switch to your branch → Rebase → Fix conflicts (if any) → Push the branch again.**
